  "finish": {
    "requirements": [
      {
        "requirement": "Rack body that can hang flat on a wall",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "A single column of three full cells (piece 0,0,0 / 0,0,1 / 0,0,2) joined by snaps. I used one column on purpose: neighbouring hex cells never share a flat back face, so only a single column puts every hanger on one wall plane."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Hangs from keyhole hangers",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "Two hex-acc-keyhole hangers on the same back face (slot 0), at the bottom and top cells (accessory:0#0,0#0 and accessory:2#0,0#0). This gives two screw points on one plane."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Hooks for small hand tools",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "Five hex-acc-hook plates with two hooks each, so 10 hooks in total. Three are on the front face opposite the wall (slot 3, one per cell). The other two are on the middle cell's front-angled faces (slots 2 and 4)."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Build passes checks",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "check_build: ok, meshClash clear, no errors. The only note is an info message that 11 open faces could take caps."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "I built a wall tool rack from three cells stacked in one column, 14 parts in total (3 cells, 2 snaps, 7 accessories). Two keyhole hangers sit on the back face, at the top and bottom cells, so it lifts onto two screws already in the wall and drops into place. Five hook plates give 10 hooks for small hand tools: three plates on the front face (one per cell) and two on the middle cell's angled front faces. The configurator check passed with no errors and no mesh clashes. This confirms the parts fit, not how much weight the rack can hold."
  },
